# Does it work in practice? Evidence, limits, and alternatives
prompt: "How well do QALY estimates agree across different elicitation methods and countries? What real-world numbers do agencies like NICE use as cost-per-QALY thresholds? What alternatives (DALYs, capability approach, healthy years equivalents) have been proposed, and why haven't they replaced QALYs?"
comments: {"repoId":"R_kgDOUIbP1A","category":"Announcements","categoryId":"DIC_kwDOUIbP1M4DEv5x"}
enroll: https://drawcast.anvil.app

## Cross-Method Agreement
level: advanced
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - TTO
      - SG
      - VAS
      - EQ-5D-5L
      - DCE
      - UK
      - US
      - Dutch
    values:
      - 0.72
      - 0.81
      - 0.65
      - 0.7
      - 0.75
      - 0.7
      - 0.78
      - 0.68
    value_labels: false
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 1
    y_label: Utility weight (u_t)

    arrow range_arrow #b5482e from.anchor top_left from.ref methods_cluster to.anchor top_right to.ref methods_cluster style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label range_label "0.65–0.81 range" above attach_to range_arrow hidden true
    line divider dashed #8f887c through [{"ref":"methods_cluster","anchor":"right"}] angle 90 hidden true
    label label_uk "0.70" above attach_to bar_6 hidden true
    label label_us "0.78" above attach_to bar_7 hidden true
    label label_dutch "0.68" above attach_to bar_8 hidden true
    group methods_cluster members ["bar_1","bar_2","bar_3","bar_4","bar_5"] hidden true
    group tariff_cluster members ["bar_6","bar_7","bar_8"] hidden true
    label label_tto "0.72" above attach_to bar_1 hidden true
    label label_sg "0.81" above attach_to bar_2 hidden true
    label label_vas "0.65" above attach_to bar_3 hidden true
    label label_methods "Methods" above attach_to methods_cluster hidden true
    label label_tariffs "Tariffs" above attach_to tariff_cluster hidden true

    card title "Cross-Method Agreement"

    camera on ["bar_1","bar_2","bar_3","bar_4","bar_5"] zoom fit

Take that hip-replacement patient's moderate pain state and ask five elicitation methods for its utility weight — you get five different numbers.
    draw axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 label_methods

Time trade-off gives about 0.72, standard gamble about 0.81, a visual analogue scale about 0.65 — same health state, same profile, different instrument.
    blocking false

    show label_tto

    point at.ref bar_1 gesture tap

    show label_sg

    point at.ref bar_2 gesture tap

    show label_vas

    point at.ref bar_3 gesture tap

    camera reset true

Swap country tariffs — UK, US, Dutch value sets — and the same EQ-5D-5L descriptor shifts again, sometimes by ten points on the 0-to-1 scale.
    draw bar_6 bar_7 bar_8 divider label_tariffs label_uk label_us label_dutch

The QALY formula stays fixed; the u_t going into it does not — which is the empirical problem this lecture takes on.
    draw range_arrow range_label

## What Agencies Actually Pay
text: {"font_size":34}
use: number_line
with:
    min: 0
    max: 200000
    step: 50000
    intervals:
      - {from: 20000, to: 30000}
      - {from: 100000, to: 150000}
      - {from: 60000, to: 90000}
    arrow_ops:
      - {from: 30000, to: 50000, label: end-of-life exception}

    label axis_title "Cost/QALY" above attach_to line hidden true
    mark cross_who around interval_2 kind cross hidden true
    label date_who "2016 — withdrawn" below attach_to interval_2 hidden true
    label label_nice "NICE" above attach_to interval_0 hidden true
    label label_icer "ICER" above attach_to interval_1 hidden true
    label label_who "WHO-CHOICE (dropped)" above attach_to interval_2 hidden true
    label label_eol "end-of-life exception" above attach_to hop_0 hidden true

    card title "What Agencies Actually Pay"

Now that u_t itself is unstable, the next question is what number policy actually multiplies it by.
    draw line tick_0 tick_50000 tick_100000 tick_150000 tick_200000 axis_title parallel true

NICE has used a £20,000-to-£30,000 threshold band since the early 2000s — spend more per QALY gained and a treatment usually gets rejected.
    draw interval_0 label_nice parallel true

End-of-life drugs get a documented exception, pushed toward £50,000, which is less a rule than an acknowledged fudge factor.
    draw hop_0 label_eol parallel true

The US never adopted a formal number — ICER's reviews informally cite $100,000 to $150,000 per QALY —
    draw interval_1 label_icer parallel true

    fade hop_0 label_eol to 0.4

and the WHO's old rule of one to three times GDP per capita was dropped in 2016 for being too crude to defend.
    draw interval_2 date_who label_who parallel true

    fade interval_2 to 0.35

    draw cross_who

## Alternatives on Offer
use: data_table
with:
    columns:
      - Method
      - Origin
      - Core difference
      - Status
    rows:
      - [DALY, 'Murray & Lopez, 1996', 'Years lost, not gained', 'Burden, not choice']
      - [ICECAP, Sen, Broader wellbeing, Hard vs. health budget]
      - [HYE, 'Mehrez & Gafni, 1989', Whole-profile trade-off, Rarely elicited]
    font_size: 32
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 50
      w: 920
      h: 620

    text bridge_note "Why a different currency?" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 420 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Alternatives on Offer"

Now that the thresholds are on the table, the natural question is why anyone would want a different currency altogether.
    draw header bridge_note parallel true

    erase bridge_note

The DALY, built for the Global Burden of Disease study by Murray and Lopez in 1996, counts years lost to disability instead of years gained in health — same arithmetic, opposite direction.
    draw row_1

Sen's capability approach asks what a person can actually do and be, not just their health state; the ICECAP instruments try to capture that, but broad wellbeing is hard to weigh against a health budget.
    draw row_2

The Healthy Years Equivalent, proposed by Mehrez and Gafni in 1989, is theoretically the most defensible — it trades off entire health profiles — and it is rarely used, because eliciting it takes two nested gambles most respondents cannot sit through.
    draw row_3

    highlight row_1__c3 row_2__c3 row_3__c3 effect underline color #b5482e duration 3

## Why the QALY Persists
level: advanced

    shape ledger_box #3d3833 shape rect x 500 y 430 width 700 height 300 hidden true
    text title_qaly "QALY" x 500 y 600 font_size 32 hidden true
    line divider dashed #8f887c through [[500,365],[500,485]] hidden true
    icon icon_disagreement #8f887c size 44 x 500 y 230 of bar-chart style.opacity 0.55 hidden true
    text label_disagreement "Method agreement" #6b655c x 500 y 185 font_size 20 style.opacity 0.75 hidden true
    icon icon_threshold #8f887c size 44 x 280 y 230 of trending-up style.opacity 0.55 hidden true
    text label_threshold "Cost-per-QALY threshold" #6b655c x 280 y 185 font_size 20 style.opacity 0.75 hidden true
    arrow arrow_threshold dashed #8f887c from.anchor top from.ref icon_threshold to.anchor bottom_left to.ref ledger_box style.opacity 0.3 hidden true
    icon icon_alternatives #8f887c size 44 x 720 y 230 of table style.opacity 0.55 hidden true
    text label_alternatives "DALYs, HYE, capability" #6b655c x 720 y 185 font_size 20 style.opacity 0.75 hidden true
    arrow arrow_alternatives dashed #8f887c from.anchor top from.ref icon_alternatives to.anchor bottom_right to.ref ledger_box style.opacity 0.3 hidden true
    text strength_header "Strength" #2f6b8f x 260 y 530 font_size 26 hidden true
    icon strength_check above-right strength_barchart gap 4 #2f6b8f size 30 of check hidden true
    icon strength_barchart #2f6b8f size 44 x 260 y 430 of bar-chart hidden true
    text strength_label "one number, all diseases" right strength_barchart gap 18 #3d3833 font_size 20 hidden true
    text weakness_header "Weakness" #b5482e x 680 y 530 font_size 26 hidden true
    text weakness_asterisk "*" above-right weakness_barchart gap 4 #b5482e font_size 34 hidden true
    icon weakness_barchart #b5482e size 44 x 680 y 430 of bar-chart hidden true
    text weakness_label "false precision" right weakness_barchart gap 18 #3d3833 font_size 20 hidden true

    card title "Why the QALY Persists"

Now that we've seen the disagreement, the price tags, and the rivals, the question is what to make of all three together.
    draw ledger_box title_qaly icon_disagreement label_disagreement icon_threshold label_threshold icon_alternatives label_alternatives arrow_threshold arrow_alternatives

    pause 0.3

The QALY's numbers wobble across methods and countries, its thresholds are administratively set rather than derived, and its rivals each solve a different problem — and it is still what gets computed before that hip replacement or that drug gets funded.
    draw divider strength_header strength_check strength_barchart strength_label weakness_header weakness_asterisk weakness_barchart weakness_label

    pause 0.3

What holds it in place is not philosophical superiority — it is thirty years of accumulated data, trained analysts, and regulatory precedent no alternative has matched.
    icon persistence_icon #8a5fa8 size 42 x 500 y 555 of layers
    text persistence_label "regulatory precedent" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 515 font_size 20

    fade arrow_threshold arrow_alternatives to 1 duration 0.6

What you can now see is a tool imperfect in exactly the ways this course has traced, and adopted anyway because the alternative to an imperfect common measure is no common measure at all.
    mark seal_mark around ledger_box title_qaly kind box
    text adopted_tag "ADOPTED" #3d3833 x 500 y 320 font_size 18

    highlight ledger_box title_qaly effect glow

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_prev_kicker "Previous" x 500 y 545 font_size 24 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_prev href lecture:4 title "The ethical debate: whose life counts, and how much?" size 320 x 500 y 395

    link end_again href lecture:5 title "Watch again" form text open here x 500 y 160
    text end_count "5 of 5" x 500 y 95 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
